<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">County Cork</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">County <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a> is the largest county in Ireland, in the south-west of the province of Munster, named after the city of Cork. Its west has a deeply indented coast and in early medieval times was home to kingdoms of the Corcu Loigde and branches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eoganachta">Eóganachta</a>. The county was created after the Norman conquest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/county-cork.jpg" alt="County Cork" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maximilian Dörrbecker (Chumwa); derivative work by Rannpháirtí anaithnid derivative work by Mabuska · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Island_of_Ireland_location_map_Cork.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>County <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a> is the largest county of Ireland by land area and the southernmost. It borders Kerry to the west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limerick">Limerick</a> to the north, Tipperary to the north-east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a> to the east. The Bandon, Blackwater and Lee and their valleys dominate the centre, and the south-western coast has many peninsulas, among them the Beara Peninsula, Sheep's Head, Mizen Head and Brow Head, the most southerly point of mainland Ireland. The county contains the southern part of the Golden Vale pastureland. It has twenty-four historic baronies, the most of any county.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Much of the county was once part of the Kingdom of Deas Mumhan, anglicised as Desmond, ruled by the MacCarthy Mór dynasty. After the Norman invasion in the 12th century the McCarthy clan were pushed west into what is now West Cork and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-kerry">County Kerry</a>, and the north and east were taken by the Hiberno-Norman FitzGerald dynasty, who became the Earls of Desmond. Cork City received an English royal charter in 1318. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desmond-rebellions">Desmond Rebellions</a> of 1569–1573 and 1579–1583 destroyed the FitzGerald dynasty, and afterwards much of the county was colonised by English settlers in the Plantation of Munster.</p><p>In 1491 Perkin Warbeck, who claimed to be Richard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrewsbury">Shrewsbury</a>, landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a> and sought support against Henry VII. The mayor and several citizens went with him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and were executed when the rebellion collapsed, and the nickname rebel county is traced to these events. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kinsale">Battle of Kinsale</a> in 1601 all but ended the rebels' hopes in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war-ireland">Nine Years' War</a>. County Cork was officially created in 1606 by dividing the older County Desmond.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-war-of-independence">Irish War of Independence</a> of 1919–1921 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a> saw considerable fighting, including the Kilmichael Ambush in November 1920 and the Crossbarry Ambush in March 1921. On 11 December 1920 fires started by the Black and Tans gutted Cork City centre. Most IRA units in Cork opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-irish-treaty">Anglo-Irish Treaty</a>, and the Irish Free State took the city and county in August 1922. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-collins">Michael Collins</a>, born near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clonakilty">Clonakilty</a>, was killed at Béal na Bláth during the civil war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The county lies in the province of Munster, and its city, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a>, is the second-largest city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-ireland">Republic of Ireland</a>. It has two Gaeltacht areas where Irish is the main everyday language, Múscraí in the north and Cape Clear Island. The port of Cobh, then called Queenstown, was the point of embarkation for many Irish emigrants and the last stop of the RMS Titanic. Blarney Castle holds the Blarney Stone.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “County Cork” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Thomas Osborne Davis, a Protestant nationalist from <b>County Cork</b>, was one of the three co-founders of Young Ireland, the movement that sought Ireland's independence from Britain.</blockquote><a href="/songs/a-nation-once-again">A Nation Once Again</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1952, collecting in the British Isles with her husband George Pickow, Ritchie met the Irish traditional singer Elizabeth Cronin in Macroom, <b>County Cork</b>, who sang a version called &quot;The Bells of Heaven&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/i-saw-three-ships">I Saw Three Ships</a></li><li><blockquote>In Irish tradition they held the kingship of Munster before the rise of the Eóganachta of Cashel around the 5th century, and afterwards kept a respected place as a free subject kingdom in south-west <b>Cork</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/corcu-loigde">Corcu Loigde</a></li><li><blockquote>Their territory covered much of later Kerry and west <b>Cork</b>, and they ruled over earlier peoples such as the Corcu Duibne.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-iarmumu">Kingdom of Iarmumu</a></li><li><blockquote>Donnubán's descendants, the O'Donovans, moved south into <b>Cork</b>, where they held lordships near Skibbereen for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/state/ui-fidgenti">Ui Fidgenti</a></li></ul></section>
